{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_43_1393709560#7_2901318458","relevance":3,"reason":"The Imperva page is dedicated to social engineering attack techniques and prevention, and the snippet directly explains that social engineers manipulate human feelings such as curiosity or fear to draw victims into traps.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to a victim by triggering feelings such as curiosity, fear, alarm, or attraction to an offer, thereby drawing the victim into a trap."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991","reason":"The result set remains highly productive for direct evidence about social-engineering cybercrime manipulating victims through emotional appeals, so continue for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_573680833#7_1162271836","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":6,"snippet":"2020] The most common social engineering attacks [updated 2020] Introduction Phishing Watering hole Whaling attack Pretexting Baiting and quid pro quo attacks Tailgating Read more about social engineering attacks Sources \" , \"segment\": \"Pretexting The term pretexting indicates the practice of presenting oneself as someone else to obtain private information. Usually, attackers create a fake identity and use it to manipulate the receipt of information. Attackers leveraging this specific <cover>social engineering technique adopt several identities they have created. This bad habit could expose their operations to the investigations conducted by security experts and law enforcement. The success of the pretexting attack heavily pretends on the ability’s attacker to build trust. Most advanced forms of pretexting attacks try to manipulate the victims </cover>into performing an action that enables an attacker to discover and exploit a point of failure inside an organization. An attacker can impersonate an external IT services operator to ask internal staff for information that could allow accessing systems within the organization. Baiting and quid pro quo attacks Another social engineering technique is the baiting that exploits the human’s curiosity. Baiting is sometimes confused with other social engineering attacks. Its main characteristic is "}}
