{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_642027397#0_1410055885","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":8,"snippet":"Share page Copy link About sharing image copyright Thinkstock image caption Hackers use social engineering - essentially tricking people into breaking normal security procedures Most of us like to think we're too clever to be caught out by email and telephone scams, but in fact any of us can get caught out by fraudsters. Their tricks have gone far beyond the infamous fax from a \"Nigerian prince\" you've never heard of asking you for money. Now frauds are increasingly sophisticated and you are much more likely to hear from someone you <cover>trust. Fraudsters dupe their victims using a type of psychological manipulation known as \"social engineering\". </cover>It is essentially a confidence trick that influences a person to take action that may not be in their best interest. With many technical security defences in place to prevent banks and companies from being hacked directly, it is we humans that represent the weak spot that criminals seek to target. To explain how they do this, security expert and self-professed 'Human Hacker' Chris Hadnagy uses the psychology behind the parent-child relationship. \"Children are little people that get us to do things we'd never thought we would ever do,\" he "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"11/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_04_642027397#0_1410055885","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to explaining social engineering scams and directly states that fraudsters dupe victims through psychological manipulation, a confidence trick that influences people to take actions not in their best interest.","learned":"For cybercrime to appeal to a victim can mean using trust and psychological manipulation to influence the victim into acting against their own interest."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991","reason":"The current social-engineering query is highly productive for direct evidence on victim manipulation and appeals, so continue for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_749830055#2_1726162605","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":9,"snippet":"Do Your Homework 3. Abstain from Clicking Links 4. Download Wisely 5. Be Cautious of Foreign Offers How to Protect Yourself from Social Engineering Attacks? Incognito Forensic Foundation (IFF Lab) – Your Awareness & Diagnostic Partner Contact us for Awareness Programs and Investigation Services. \" , \"segment\": \"Rightly called ‘social engineering’ as it exploits a person’s natural tendency to <cover>trust another individual. Social Engineering is more of an art! Know why? Because it requires a certain mastery to manipulate people into entrusting another individual, usually a stranger, with personal information. Miscreants find it easier to employ social engineering to gain personal information than use traditional techniques such as hacking. Social engineering attack techniques vary as per the final objectives of miscreants. Some may seek information such as passwords and financial details to cheat victims </cover>of money. Likewise, some use this technique to gain access to the victim’s computer to covertly install malicious software. This enables them to gain control over their systems and access their personal information. The Crux of Social Engineering Attacks Trust is crucial for an individual’s security –in the physical, emotional space as well as cyber space!\" , \"start_char"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"12/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_749830055#2_1726162605","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to social engineering attacks and directly explains that they exploit a person's natural tendency to trust and manipulate people into entrusting personal information to attackers, which directly supports what it means for cybercrime to appeal to a victim.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to victims by exploiting their natural tendency to trust others, making them hand over personal, financial, or access information."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991","reason":"This query continues to return direct grade-3 evidence about cybercrime using trust, emotions, and psychological manipulation to appeal to victims, so continue through the result set."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_348276539#18_539427361","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":10,"snippet":"Updates (they’re more important than you might think) 7. Stay Off Public WiFi (use an envelope, not a postcard!) 8. Beware Social Engineering (don’t let them in your head!) Keep Your Company Information Secure \" , \"segment\": \"No matter how hard the mail carrier squints while holding it up to the light, they won’t see what’s in it. 8. Beware Social Engineering (don’t let them in your head!) <cover>Social engineering refers to a broad spectrum of malicious activities using psychological manipulation to trick users into giving away sensitive information. Perpetrators are particularly patient, waiting in the weeds, collecting data and background information on their intended victims. Then they gain the victim’s trust </cover>and provide seemingly harmless reasons for their victims to give up sensitive information. What makes social engineering so dangerous is that it preys on human error, much more of a wild card—and much harder to track—than taking advantage of vulnerabilities in software and operating systems. Social Engineering bad guys try to get at users through human psychology and preying on curiosity. It’s important to go into all cyber-situations with your eyes wide open because "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"13/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_348276539#18_539427361","relevance":3,"reason":"The result directly defines social engineering as malicious activity using psychological manipulation to trick users into giving away sensitive information, gaining the victim's trust and preying on curiosity. This directly supports what it means for a cybercrime to appeal to a victim.","learned":"A cybercrime may appeal to a victim by using psychology, trust, and curiosity to make a request seem harmless and induce disclosure of sensitive information."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991","reason":"The social-engineering query is still producing direct grade-3 explanations of how cybercrime appeals to victims through psychology, trust, and emotion, so continue."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_192987513#1_356912055","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":11,"snippet":"You Need to Know What Is Social Engineering? The Most Common Social Engineering Tactics 1) Phishing & Spear Phishing 2) Pretexting 3) Baiting 4) Email Hacking If you have been hacked, follow these steps to recover your account. 5) Quid Pro Quo 6) Phones & Vishing 7) Tailgating 8) Social Media 9) Rogue Security 10) Emergency Language Protect Yourself Against Social Engineering Attacks \" , \"segment\": \"what is social engineering? What Is Social Engineering? <cover>Social engineering uses psychology to manipulate your most vulnerable cybersecurity element — the human. Social engineers target your emotions and relationships to gain access to valuable information and your network. These can be one-off attacks, or some social engineers take the time to formulate relationships with their victim </cover>making them even more dangerous. Social engineering often takes less time than automated software. Getting a human to reply with a password or click a link takes minutes, while trying to crack a password that follows the most up to date security measures can take years. Oftentimes social engineers will combine more than one tactic for a single attack, which makes it even trickier and more important to identify a social engineering attack. For example, the "}}
