{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_1678228016#5_2927236815","relevance":2,"reason":"The flashcard result is related and gives direct examples of social-engineering principles such as urgency, authority, and intimidation used to manipulate a victim into disclosing confidential data, but it is a practice-test/Quizlet page rather than a dedicated explanatory evidence document.","learned":"Social-engineering appeals can use urgency, authority, and intimidation to pressure victims into compliance."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991","reason":"Although the latest result was weaker, this result set has produced many direct grade-3 explanations of cybercrime appeals to victims, so continue a little further for additional strong documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_192987513#0_356910185","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":15,"snippet":"expert will tell you: it doesn’t matter how many locks you have on the door, if someone lets an attack inside. In fact, social engineering attacks account for 33% of data breaches, reported the 2019 Data Breach Investigations Report by Verizon. The goal of social engineering is to manipulate you to let them into the network, so they can infect it with malware or comb your data for valuable information. Before we can dive into the top 10 social engineering tactics, we have to answer the question: what is social engineering? What Is Social Engineering? <cover>Social engineering uses psychology to manipulate your most vulnerable cybersecurity element — the human. Social engineers target your emotions and relationships to gain access to valuable information and your network. These can be one-off attacks, or some social engineers take the time to formulate relationships with their victim </cover>making them even more dangerous.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"The Top 10 Social Engineering Tactics You Need to Know\" , \"url\": \"https://www.accesssystems.com/blog/the-top-10-social-engineering-tactics-you-need-to-know\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"18/50"}}
