{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1246643094#6_2667012999","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another Fortinet social-engineering segment, but it directly explains that spear phishing researches how to manipulate the victim, water holing infects sites victims trust and feel safe on, and quid pro quo offers something in exchange for information or action.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to a victim by using trusted contexts, victim-specific research, or an apparent exchange to manipulate the victim into revealing information or taking an action."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991","reason":"The result set continues to yield direct grade-3 evidence about cybercrime appealing to victims through trust, curiosity, urgency, and psychological manipulation, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_749830055#1_1726160666","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":24,"snippet":"5. Be Cautious of Foreign Offers How to Protect Yourself from Social Engineering Attacks? Incognito Forensic Foundation (IFF Lab) – Your Awareness & Diagnostic Partner Contact us for Awareness Programs and Investigation Services. \" , \"segment\": \"It is a technique used by cyber criminals to defraud innocent victims. Here’s all that you need to know about social engineering attack techniques. Also, some handy tips to avoid being a social engineering <cover>victim. What is Social Engineering? Social Engineering is the trending technique for trapping gullible individuals into divulging their personal information. Rightly called ‘social engineering’ as it exploits a person’s natural tendency to trust another individual. Social Engineering is more of an art! Know why? Because it requires a certain mastery to manipulate </cover>people into entrusting another individual, usually a stranger, with personal information. Miscreants find it easier to employ social engineering to gain personal information than use traditional techniques such as hacking.\" , \"start_char\": 522.000000 , \"title\": \"Top 5 Social Engineering Attack Techniques Used by Cyber Criminals\" , \"url\": \"https://ifflab.org/top-5-social-engineering-attack-techniques-used-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"27/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_749830055#1_1726160666","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the IFF Lab social engineering page already seen, but it directly explains that social engineering is used by cybercriminals to trap victims by exploiting their natural tendency to trust and manipulating them into divulging personal information.","learned":"Repeated evidence: cybercrime can appeal to victims by exploiting trust and gullibility so they entrust personal information to a criminal."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991","reason":"The result set is still returning direct grade-3 evidence about cybercrime appeals to victims through trust, emotion, and psychological manipulation, so continue for additional relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_958868178#0_1970006388","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":25,"snippet":"end_char\": 873.000000 , \"headings\": \"Pretexting: The Art of Impersonation Understanding the Threat Pretexting: The Art of Impersonation Patrick Putman What is Pretexting Impersonation Tailgating/Piggybacking Conclusion Leave a Comment \" , \"segment\": \"Pretexting: The Art of Impersonation | United States Cybersecurity Magazine Understanding the Threat Pretexting: The Art of Impersonation Patrick Putman What is Pretexting Pretexting is a <cover>social engineering tactic that uses deception and false motives. Simply put, pretexting crafts fictional situations to obtain personal, sensitive, or privileged information. Pretexting often involves researching the target prior to the attack. The data collected is then used to manipulate and deceive the victim. The main goal of the threat actor is to gain the target’s trust </cover>and exploit it for financial or political gain. A pretext attack can occur in person or over the phone. Impersonation Impersonation is a tactic used by pretexters to deceive the target. Playing the role of a trusted individual, they manipulate their victim into granting them access to a facility or system.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Pretexting: The Art "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"28/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_958868178#0_1970006388","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to pretexting, a social-engineering cybercrime tactic, and directly explains that attackers craft fictional situations, research targets, manipulate and deceive victims, and gain trust to obtain sensitive information or access.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to a victim by using a tailored false pretext and trusted impersonation to deceive the victim into sharing sensitive information or granting access."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991","reason":"The social-engineering result set is still producing direct grade-3 evidence about cybercrime appealing to victims through deception, trust, emotions, and psychological manipulation, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1588936864#4_3284475252","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":26,"snippet":"have picked up on certain behaviours and be there lurking in the wings to steal the required badges and access cards etc. In another form of psychological manipulation, the criminal may seek as well to befriend employees during a coffee break and over the course of a few weeks gain their trust and confidence so they can eventually gain access to the secured areas to eventually betray that very same <cover>trust and confidence. Such a simple form of social engineering attack can make a mockery of the high-end expensive electronic, software-based entry systems and regulations of an organisation and affects all enterprises whatever their size. 3. Tailgating Psychology Tailgating is another form of psychological manipulation as it is carried out with a view to making the victim </cover>carry out a specific action the criminal wants them in order to execute a fraudulent, malicious act that can lead to a data breach causing untold damage both financially and reputationally. Like a phishing attack including spear-phishing or whaling, it is an information security confidence trick designed to fool people with authorisation to allow those who have no authorisation to gain access to restricted areas and information. Tailgating Psychology 4. "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"29/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1588936864#4_3284475252","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to tailgating/social engineering and directly explains that the criminal uses psychological manipulation, trust, and confidence to make the victim carry out a specific action that enables a fraudulent or malicious act.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to a victim by building trust and confidence, then exploiting that relationship to induce the victim to perform an action the criminal wants."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991","reason":"This result set continues to produce direct grade-3 evidence about cybercrime appealing to victims through trust, confidence, emotion, and psychological manipulation, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_43_1393709560#0_2901304197","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":27,"snippet":"social engineering Social engineering attack techniques Baiting Scareware Pretexting Phishing Spear phishing See how Imperva Web Application Firewall can help you with social engineering attacks. Social engineering prevention \" , \"segment\": \"What is Social Engineering | Attack Techniques & Prevention Methods | Imperva Social Engineering What is social engineering Social engineering is the term used for a broad range of malicious activities accomplished through human interactions. It uses <cover>psychological manipulation to trick users into making security mistakes or giving away sensitive information. Social engineering attacks happen in one or more steps. A perpetrator first investigates the intended victim to gather necessary background information, such as potential points of entry and weak security protocols, needed to proceed with the attack. Then, the attacker moves to gain the victim’s trust </cover>and provide stimuli for subsequent actions that break security practices, such as revealing sensitive information or granting access to critical resources. Social Engineering Attack Lifecycle What makes social engineering especially dangerous is that it relies on human error, rather than vulnerabilities in software and operating systems. Mistakes made by legitimate users are much less predictable, making them harder to identify and thwart than a malware-based intrusion. Social engineering attack "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"30/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_43_1393709560#0_2901304197","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the Imperva social engineering page already seen, but it is dedicated to social engineering and directly defines it as psychological manipulation that tricks users into security mistakes or disclosure, after researching the victim and gaining their trust.","learned":"Repeated evidence: cybercrime appeals to victims by researching them, gaining trust, and using psychological stimuli to make them reveal sensitive information or grant access."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991","reason":"The social-engineering result set is still yielding direct grade-3 evidence on how cybercrime appeals to victims through trust, psychology, and emotional manipulation, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1169004813#1_1285036297","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":28,"snippet":"subject. See also Frown Inattention Smile External links The dictionary definition of glare at Wiktionary v t e<cover>Psychological manipulation Rewarding: pleasant ( positive reinforcement) Attention Bribery Child grooming Flattery Gifts Ingratiation Love bombing Nudging Praise Seduction Smiling Superficial charm Superficial sympathy Aversive: unpleasant ( positive punishment) Anger Character assassination Crying Emotional blackmail Fearmongering Frowning Glaring Guilt trip Inattention Intimidation Nagging Nit-picking criticism Passive aggression Relational aggression Sadism Screaming Shaming Silent treatment Social rejection Swearing Threats Victim blaming Victim playing Intermittent or partial negative reinforcement Climate of fear Traumatic bonding Other techniques Bait-and-switch Deception Denial Deplatforming Deprogramming Disinformation Distortion Distraction Divide and rule Double bind Entrapment Evasion Exaggeration Gaslighting Good cop/bad cop Indoctrination Low-balling Lying Minimisation Moving the goalposts Pride-and-ego down Rationalization Reid technique Setting up to fail Triangulation Trojan horse You are either with us, or against us Contexts Abuse Abusive power and control Advertising Brainwashing Bullying Catholic guilt Confidence trick Guilt–shame–fear spectrum of cultures Interrogation Jewish mother stereotype Media manipulation Mind games Mobbing Moral panic Propaganda Salesmanship Scapegoating Smear campaign Social engineering (</cover>blagging) Spin Suggestibility Whispering campaign Related topics Antisocial personality disorder Assertiveness Blame Borderline personality disorder Carrot and stick Dumbing "}}
