{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1169004813#1_1285036297","relevance":1,"reason":"The passage is a broad list of psychological manipulation techniques and contexts, with only a passing mention of social engineering; it is not dedicated to cybercrime or explaining how cybercrime appeals to victims.","learned":"Broad psychological-manipulation pages are likely less useful unless they explicitly connect the manipulation to cybercrime/social engineering victims."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ phishing \"appeals to\" (+ victim \"victim's\") (+ curiosity fear urgency trust))","reason":"Search for exact phrasing about phishing appeals to a victim's curiosity, fear, urgency, or trust, which should retrieve focused explanations of what such an appeal means in cybercrime."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ phishing \"appeals to\" (+ victim \"victim's\") (+ curiosity fear urgency trust))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1246643094#4_2667007090","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1992","rank":2,"snippet":"A baiting attack attempts to draw in a victim by promising something that appeals to their sense of curiosity or greed. This lures the target into installing or clicking on something that ends up putting malware, such as that used for pharming or spyware, onto their system. Scareware Scareware bombards a target with fake threats or false alarms in the hopes that their natural inclination to protect themselves or something they value drives them to taking the desired action. One of the more common types is using realistic-looking banners warning that their computer may be infected with a virus or some other kind of malware. Pretexting In an attack that uses pretexting, the attacker lies to the victim regarding their identity. After they have gained the target’s trust, they trick them into handing over sensitive information. Phishing In a <cover>phishing attack, the attacker creates a sense of urgency or appeals to the victim’</cover>s curiosity.\" , \"start_char\": 2259.000000 , \"title\": \"What Is Social Engineering? Preventing Social Engineering Attacks | Fortinet\" , \"url\": \"https://www.fortinet.com/resources/cyberglossary/social-engineering\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1246643094#5_2667010111","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"32/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1246643094#4_2667007090","relevance":3,"reason":"This Fortinet social-engineering page segment directly states that baiting promises something appealing to curiosity or greed, scareware exploits protective instincts, pretexting gains trust, and phishing creates urgency or appeals to the victim's curiosity. It directly supports what it means for cybercrime to appeal to a victim.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to victims by promising something that triggers curiosity or greed, creating urgency, exploiting protective instincts, or gaining trust to induce clicks, downloads, or disclosure."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1992","reason":"The exact-phrasing query is producing direct evidence about phishing and baiting appealing to victim curiosity, greed, urgency, and trust, so continue for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1992"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1246643094#3_2667004142","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1992","rank":3,"snippet":"<cover>Phishing Water Holing Quid Pro Quo Honey Trap Tailgating Rogue Vishing Well-known Examples of Social Engineering Attacks Tips To Prevent Social Engineering Attacks Safe Communication and Account Management Habits Never Click on Links in an Email or Message Multi-factor Authentication (MFA) Using Strong Passwords and a Password Manager Be Cautious of Building Online-only Friendships Safe Network Use Habits Never Let Strangers Connect to Your Primary Wi-Fi Network Use a VPN Keep All Network-connected Devices and Services Secure Safe Device Use Habits Use Comprehensive Internet Security Software Do Not Ever Leave Your Devices Unsecured in Public Keep All Software Updated Check for Known Data Breaches of Your Online Accounts How Fortinet Can Help FAQs What is social engineering? How does social engineering work? What are the well-known examples of social engineering attacks? Learn more about Fortinet today \" , \"segment\": \"Social engineering attackers abuse this tendency by offering advice, something exclusive, or personalizing their offer to make the target feel obliged to give something back. Commitment After someone commits to a course of action, they feel obligated to stick with their decision. An attacker using social engineering tools can exploit this </cover>"}}
