{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ phishing (+ \"human emotions\" \"emotional triggers\" emotions) (+ victim victims) (+ urgency fear curiosity greed))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1209548617#8_2463468724","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1993","rank":1,"snippet":"their victims; in others, the kill chain is automated, typically activated by the user clicking on a link to visit a malicious website or execute malicious code. Top 5 social engineering techniques According to the InfoSec Institute, the following five techniques are among the most commonly used social engineering attacks. 1. Phishing In a phishing attack, an attacker uses a message sent by email, social media, instant messaging clients or SMS to obtain sensitive information from a victim or trick them into clicking a link to a malicious website. <cover>Phishing messages get a victim’s attention and call to action by arousing curiosity, asking for help, or pulling other emotional triggers. </cover>They often use logos, images or text styles to spoof an organization’s identity, making it seem that the message originates from a work colleague, the victim’s bank, or other official channel. Most phishing messages use a sense of urgency, causing the victim to believe there will be negative consequences if they don’t surrender sensitive information quickly. 2. Watering hole A watering hole attack involves launching or downloading malicious code from a legitimate website, which is commonly visited by the targets of the attack.\" , \""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"34/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1209548617#8_2463468724","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to social engineering techniques and directly explains that phishing messages get a victim's attention and call to action by arousing curiosity, asking for help, or pulling other emotional triggers, often adding urgency to obtain sensitive information or clicks.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to a victim by grabbing attention and prompting action through curiosity, helpfulness, emotional triggers, spoofed trust, and urgency."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993","reason":"This query produced strong direct evidence about phishing using emotional triggers to get victims' attention and action, so continue for more grade-3 documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1209548617#9_2463470316","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1993","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1209548617#9_2463470316\" , \"end_char\": 8504.000000 , \"headings\": \" \" , \"segment\": \"<cover>Phishing messages get a victim’s attention and call to action by arousing curiosity, asking for help, or pulling other emotional triggers. </cover>They often use logos, images or text styles to spoof an organization’s identity, making it seem that the message originates from a work colleague, the victim’s bank, or other official channel. Most phishing messages use a sense of urgency, causing the victim to believe there will be negative consequences if they don’t surrender sensitive information quickly. 2. Watering hole A watering hole attack involves launching or downloading malicious code from a legitimate website, which is commonly visited by the targets of the attack. For example, attackers might compromise a financial industry news site, knowing that individuals who work in finance and thus represent an attractive target, are likely to visit this site. The compromised site typically installs a backdoor trojan that allows the attacker to compromise and remotely control the victim’s device. Watering hole attacks are "}}
