{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_1209548617#9_2463470316","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the same social-engineering techniques document, but it directly explains that phishing messages appeal to victims by arousing curiosity, asking for help, pulling emotional triggers, spoofing trusted identities, and creating urgency so victims click or surrender information.","learned":"Repeated evidence: phishing appeals to victims by using curiosity, helpfulness, emotional triggers, trusted spoofing, and urgency to get attention and prompt action."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993","reason":"The query is producing direct grade-3 evidence about phishing emotional triggers and victim action, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_409947886#1_858248089","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1993","rank":4,"snippet":"today! Get the best in cybersecurity, delivered to your inbox. \" , \"segment\": \"While that makes it sound like an old-fashioned scam, vishing attacks have high-tech elements: they involve automated voice simulation technology, for instance, or the scammer may use personal information about the victim harvested from earlier cyberattacks to put them at ease. No matter what technology is used, the setup for the attack follows a familiar social engineering script: An attacker creates a scenario to prey on human <cover>emotions, commonly greed or fear, and convinces the victim to disclose sensitive information, like credit card numbers or passwords. In that sense, vishing techniques mirror the phishing </cover>scams that have been around since the 1990s. But vishing calls exploit the fact that we're more likely to trust a human voice — and may target the elderly and technophobic who are naive and have no experience with these types of scams. Vishing statistics These notable numbers offer a sense of the state of vishing and why it can be a lucrative business for attackers. Vishing attacks have been on the rise over the past few years. In 2018, scam calls represented nearly 30% of all ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1588958815#3_3284524925","rank":3,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"36/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_409947886#1_858248089","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to vishing/phishing-style cybercrime and directly explains that attackers create scenarios to prey on human emotions such as greed or fear, convincing victims to disclose sensitive information like credit card numbers or passwords.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to victims by creating a scenario that preys on emotions such as greed or fear and uses trust in a communication channel to convince disclosure of sensitive data."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993","reason":"The current query is yielding direct grade-3 evidence about phishing/vishing using emotional triggers and human emotions to manipulate victims, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_409947886#0_858246067","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1993","rank":5,"snippet":"sensitive personal information over the phone. By Josh Fruhlinger CSO | May 18, 2020 7:40 am PDT Tero Vesalainen / WhataWin / Getty Images What is vishing? Vishing is a form of attack that attempts to trick victims into giving up sensitive personal information over the phone. While that makes it sound like an old-fashioned scam, vishing attacks have high-tech elements: they involve automated voice simulation technology, for instance, or the scammer may use personal information about the victim harvested from earlier cyberattacks to put them at ease. No matter what technology is used, the setup for the attack follows a familiar social engineering script: An attacker creates a scenario to prey on human <cover>emotions, commonly greed or fear, and convinces the victim to disclose sensitive information, like credit card numbers or passwords. In that sense, vishing techniques mirror the phishing </cover>scams that have been around since the 1990s.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"What is vishing? How voice phishing scams victims | CSO Online\" , \"url\": \"https://www.csoonline.com/article/3543771/vishing-explained-how-voice-phishing-attacks-scam-victims.html\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"37/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_409947886#0_858246067","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the CSO vishing article already seen, but it is dedicated to voice-phishing cybercrime and directly explains that attackers create scenarios to prey on human emotions such as greed or fear and convince victims to disclose sensitive information.","learned":"Repeated evidence: vishing/phishing appeals to victims by preying on emotions such as greed or fear and using a plausible scenario to obtain passwords, credit-card numbers, or other sensitive information."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993","reason":"The query is still producing direct grade-3 evidence about cybercrime using emotional triggers and human emotions to manipulate victims, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1588958815#2_3284522278","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1993","rank":6,"snippet":"in the same sector as the victim’s. These phishing emails look like the real things with the right logo, corporate design ,the right names, correct email addresses, job titles right down to the right social media links such as Instagram, Facebook and LinkedIn. Common business jargon and terminology will be inserted into the <cover>phishing emails as well to fool the victim into accepting it as an everyday, normal email with nothing to be feared. What are Whaling Techniques? Whaling relies heavily on the psychological manipulation behind Social Engineering to take certain actions, which can be harmful to the individual and their organization A CFO in the midst of a corporate takeover battle will have his defenses down due to stress and the cyber attacker will be aware of their stress and behaviors at this key strategic moment preying on the human emotions of uncertainty, greed, </cover>fear and urgency. With carefully selected phishing emails, the scammer can take advantage and strike to gain access to corporate systems and networks dealing a potentially fatal data breach in the information security perimeter and attack surface area.\" , \"start_char\": 2332.000000 , \"title\":"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"38/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1588958815#2_3284522278","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the whaling/phishing page already seen, but it is dedicated to phishing/whaling techniques and directly explains that attackers make emails seem normal and trustworthy while using psychological manipulation and preying on stress, uncertainty, greed, fear, and urgency to induce harmful actions.","learned":"Repeated evidence: cybercrime appeals to victims by making a lure appear ordinary or trusted while exploiting emotions such as stress, uncertainty, greed, fear, and urgency."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993","reason":"The current query continues to yield direct grade-3 evidence about phishing/vishing/whaling using emotional triggers and human emotions to manipulate victims, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_412388977#3_853159308","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1993","rank":7,"snippet":"would people who send phishing emails be excited about a natural disaster or health scare? Unsure whether an email is real or phishing? Which of the following should you do? \" , \"segment\": \"The message is made to look as though it comes from a trusted sender. If it fools the <cover>victim, he or she is coaxed into providing confidential information--often on a scam website. Sometimes malware is also downloaded onto the target's computer. Cybercriminals start by identifying a group of individuals they want to target. Then they create email and text messages that appear to be legitimate but actually contain dangerous links, attachments, or lures that trick their targets into taking an unknown, risky action. In brief: Phishers frequently use emotions like fear, curiosity, urgency, and greed to compel recipients to open attachments or click on links. Phishing </cover>attacks are designed to appear to come from legitimate companies and individuals. Cybercriminals are continuously innovating and becoming more and more sophisticated. It only takes one successful phishing attack to compromise your network and steal your data, which is why it is always important to Think Before You Click.\" , \"start_char\""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"39/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_412388977#3_853159308","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to phishing/cybercrime awareness and directly explains that phishing lures appear to come from trusted senders and use emotions such as fear, curiosity, urgency, and greed to compel victims to click links, open attachments, or provide confidential information.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to a victim by making a message seem legitimate and using emotional triggers such as fear, curiosity, urgency, or greed to compel risky actions."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993","reason":"The query is still producing direct grade-3 evidence about phishing attacks appealing to victims through trusted lures and emotional triggers, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1480356709#8_3061704748","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1993","rank":9,"snippet":"Websites What are scam website? How does a scam website work? How does a scam website exploit you? Types of scam websites Phishing Scam Websites Online Shopping Scam Websites Scareware Scam Websites Sweepstakes Scam Websites Examples of scam websites COVID-19 Vaccine Trial Scam Websites DMV <cover>Phishing Scam Websites How to identify fake websites How to avoid scam websites Check the domain name Be careful how you pay Too good to be true? Do an internet search Always use a secure connection What to do if you become a victim of a scam website How to report scam websites \" , \"segment\": \"Attractive promises such as free gift cards or a rapid wealth-building scheme can trigger optimism that may lead you to overlook any potential downsides. Fear: False virus infections and account alerts lead to panicked action that often ties in with feelings of urgency. Whether these emotions </cover>work in tandem or alone, they each serve to promote the attacker's goals. However, a scam can only exploit you if it feels relevant or relatable to you. Many variants of online scam sites exist specifically for this reason. Types of scam websites Scam websites, like ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_532668797#1_1078160918","rank":8,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"40/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1480356709#8_3061704748","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to scam/phishing websites and directly explains that they exploit victims through attractive promises, optimism, fear, false alerts, urgency, and relevance/relatability to promote the attacker's goals.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to victims by making scams feel relevant or relatable and using attractive promises or fear-based alerts to trigger optimism, panic, or urgency."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993","reason":"The query is still yielding direct grade-3 evidence about cybercrime using emotional triggers and appealing promises to manipulate victims, so continue for more relevant documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1588924746#6_3284445793","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1993","rank":10,"snippet":"and to return it to them, a significant minority admitted to wanting to keep it for themselves. This leads to a small but highly worrying potential data breach and exposes a highly vulnerable gap in the attack surface and as a result, increased cyber risk and danger to information security. This confirmed the suspicions of many in the security world who reported that unsuspecting users can be coerced through curiosity, greed etc. into picking up unknown devices and becoming <cover>victims of social engineering methods. This leads to their organization becoming vulnerable to cyber attacks, data breaches and ransomware campaigns potentially costing millions of dollars and untold, unrepairable reputational damage. 5. Techniques to Prevent Baiting Scammers who use baiting techniques know very well how to play with our fears and emotions. When you do receive an email that promotes feelings of fear, greed etc. be careful and alert – act calmly and think slowly, do not be rash with your actions.\" , \"start_char\": 5081.000000 , \"title\": \"Baiting - Anti-phishing </cover>Solution and Security Awareness Training - Keepnet Labs\" , \"url\": \"https://www.keepnetlabs.com/baiting/\" }"}}
