{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"social engineering\" \"lure victims\" (+ fear greed curiosity urgency trust))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_749830055#6_1726171159","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1994","rank":1,"snippet":"the Cyber Space 2. Phishing Example: 3. Scareware Example: 4. Pretexting Example: 5. Quid Pro Quo Example: Tips to Avoid Being a Social Engineering Victim 1. Be Slow & Steady 2. Do Your Homework 3. Abstain from Clicking Links 4. Download Wisely 5. Be Cautious of Foreign Offers How to Protect Yourself from Social Engineering Attacks? Incognito Forensic Foundation (IFF Lab) – Your Awareness & Diagnostic Partner Contact us for Awareness Programs and Investigation Services. \" , \"segment\": \"But, before that, here are some common <cover>social engineering attack techniques. 1. Baiting This technique exploits the victim’s tendency to react to a bait that attracts his/her greed or curiosity. Miscreants lure victims </cover>into a trap with the intention of stealing their personal information or infecting their systems with malware. It is possible to execute baiting in both the physical as well as cyber space. Example: (a) Baiting in the Physical Space A typical example of such social engineering attacks involves using malware-infected flash drives as the bait. Social engineers usually leave them in a place where a victim is most likely to notice it. This includes places such as elevators, parking lot and bathrooms. Most times, the "}}
