{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_899106232#3_1664502258","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to social engineering cybercrime and directly explains that attackers use convincing fake stories to lure victims into traps, including fake account-problem emails, malicious attachments framed as enjoyable or needed, administrator impersonation, prize claims, and promises of money to obtain passwords, credit-card data, bank details, or malware execution.","learned":"Cybercrime can appeal to victims by presenting convincing fake stories, useful or enjoyable files, authority requests, prizes, or money promises that lure them into revealing sensitive information or installing malware."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1994","reason":"Continue the focused social-engineering lure result set, which has been producing direct grade-3 evidence about how cybercrime appeals to victims through bait, fake stories, rewards, curiosity, greed, and trust."}}
